// Runtime/ArrayRuntime.maxon — the `Array` value representation (P1.7 slice 1) and the native runtime
// functions an array's storage substrate needs. The TRIVIAL-element core: elements are int / Byte /
// ranged-int / bool (every one ≤ 8 bytes and self-owning), so nothing walks the elements on grow, drop,
// insert or remove — `element_destroy@40` is always 0 at this slice.
//
// ⭐ THE SHAPE IS `StringRuntime`'s: one fused, `__mm_alloc`'d backing record whose fields the runtime
// functions read and the lowering writes, plus a set of `__`-prefixed Std runtime functions built as
// `StdOp` graphs (so they get register allocation, frames and the whole backend for free), installed
// on demand from `RuntimeUsage`. It DIVERGES from `String` in the one place `Array` must: a String's
// `element_size` is a compile-time constant 1, an Array's is a RUNTIME field (`element_size@24`) — the
// first runtime-polymorphic element size in shv2 — so every helper reads it out of the record and moves
// exactly that many bytes per element. A single set of helpers therefore serves every element type.
//
// ⚠ THE ARRAY IS TWO ALLOCATIONS, not one: the 48-byte record (`__mm_alloc`, a managed box with the MM
// header + refcount before it) and a SEPARATE external element buffer (`buffer@0` → a raw `__mm_alloc`'d
// span, destructor 0). This mirrors a DETACHED String, not the inline one — an array grows, so its bytes
// cannot live behind the record. `__arr_decref` releases both.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THAT SPLIT IS WHAT MAKES A ZERO-COPY SLICE POSSIBLE (P1.7 COW slice B).** Because the element
// buffer is its OWN `__mm_alloc` allocation, it has its OWN refcount word, and a non-zero count there says
// exactly one thing: **someone is viewing these bytes.** Both reference compilers refcount the RECORD
// instead, where a count of 2 cannot tell "two owners of this array" from "someone is viewing my buffer" —
// which is why the bootstrap gives up and eager-copies an owned slice outright
// (`MaxonToStandardConversion.Strings.cs:1480`: "heap-allocated source cannot be zero-copy because
// struct-level COW can't distinguish slice refs from normal refs"). shv2 is MOVE-ONLY, so a RECORD never
// has two owners and the BUFFER's count is unambiguous. `.slice()`/`.clone()` are therefore O(1) — a fresh
// 48-byte record over the parent's bytes — and `__arr_cow_detach` restores independence at the first write,
// in BOTH directions (the viewer's and the owner's).

// --- The fused 48-byte Array backing record ---
//
// Every field is an 8-byte slot; the six offsets are 0/8/16/24/32/40 and the record is 48 bytes. It is a
// DISTINCT record type from a String's (whose slot@40 is `isAscii`); do not reinterpret one as the other.
export let ArrBufferOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ArrLengthOffset = 8 as ByteOffset
export let ArrCapacityOffset = 16 as ByteOffset
export let ArrElementSizeOffset = 24 as ByteOffset
export let ArrParentOffset = 32 as ByteOffset
export let ArrElementDestroyOffset = 40 as ByteOffset
export let ArrRecordBytes = 48 as ByteCount

// ⭐ **`capacity@16` AND `parent@32` CARRY THE BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING, WHICH IS NOT THIS FILE'S TO
// DEFINE — `BufferOwnership` owns it, and the String record answers the same two questions the same
// way.** The sign of `capacity@16` says whether the buffer is this record's to write and to release;
// `parent@32` is the address `__mm_alloc` RETURNED for the allocation this record owes a release to
// (`NoOwedAllocation` for none), so `__mm_decref` finds its header just below it. It is deliberately NOT
// the parent RECORD and NOT the view's own `buffer@0`: `buffer@0` is an INTERIOR pointer
// (`base + start·element_size`) whose `-8` word is the parent's DATA, not a refcount, and a parent record
// would make a CHAIN. ⭐ **NESTED SLICES FLATTEN**: a slice of a view stores the same base and increfs it
// again, so nothing ever walks a chain and a view's lifetime never depends on the record it was cut from.
// (Both references flatten too — the bootstrap stores `source.parentPtr`, not `source`.)
//
// `element_destroy@40` is the per-element destructor a managed-element array carries (P1.7 slice 3); for a
// TRIVIAL element it is 0 and the drop path releases the buffer without walking. Both come pre-zeroed from
// `__mm_alloc`, so `__arr_create` never writes them — the same zeroing contract `__mm_alloc` relies on.
let TrivialElementDestroy = 0 as ParsedInt

// --- Byte-string literal (`b"…"`, P1.7 slice 2) ---
//
// A `b"…"` literal is the Array analogue of a String literal: a HEAP 48-byte record over an IMMORTAL
// `.rdata` byte blob. Its `capacity@16` is `RdataBufferCapacity` — the shared encoding's negative value
// for "this buffer is not the record's to write or to release" — written by the byte-string literal
// lowering (`LowerMaxonToStd`) and read by `emitBufferNotOwned` alone, never compared against.
// `element_size@24` is 1 (a byte is one byte, so the blob is byte-packed and `.get(i)` strides by 1 —
// UNLIKE a slice-1 `Array with Byte` whose ranged-alias element still occupies a machine word). The
// record is a real `__mm_alloc` box so the value is a first-class owned `Array with Byte` the ownership
// model drops through `__arr_decref` — which, on that capacity, frees the record and skips the blob.
export let ByteStringElementSize = 1 as ParsedInt

// ⭐ **WHERE AN ARRAY RECORD KEEPS THE BUFFER-OWNERSHIP ENCODING** (`BufferOwnership`) — the three slots
// its two predicates read, built from THIS file's offsets so the shared question is asked over the layout
// declared here. The zero-copy view (`.slice`/`.clone`, P1.7 COW slice B) is the second non-owned-buffer
// kind it answers for: a view is a record of its own (its own `length@8`, `element_size@24`,
// `element_destroy@40`) whose `buffer@0` points INTO another allocation's bytes at
// `base + start·element_size`, whose `capacity@16` is `ViewBufferCapacity` and whose `parent@32` is that
// allocation's BASE.
function arrayBufferSlots() returns BufferRecordSlots
	return BufferRecordSlots.create(ArrBufferOffset, capacityOffset: ArrCapacityOffset, parentOffset: ArrParentOffset)
end 'arrayBufferSlots'

// The `.rdata` label prefix a byte-string literal's blob registers under. `__`-prefixed so it never
// collides with a user name and stays out of the spec goldens (`isRuntimeFunction`'s data twin), and
// distinct from `__str_blob_` so a reader of the section can tell the two apart.
let ByteArrayBlobPrefix = "__barr_blob_"

// Every blob is padded to an 8-byte multiple, the same reason `StringRuntime.stringBlobBytes` pads: a
// future arm64 record fixup links its rebased slots by a 4-byte-granular delta, so keeping the rdata
// cursor 8-aligned across the section keeps every following slot encodable. The padding bytes sit BEYOND
// `length@8`, so no Array read (which strides `[0, length)`) ever observes them, and it is free on x64.
let ByteArrayBlobAlignment = 8 as ByteCount
let ByteArrayBlobPadByte = 0 as Byte

// Register a byte-string literal's payload as an immortal `.rdata` blob and return its label. The bytes
// are ALREADY decoded (escapes resolved, one Latin-1 byte per source codepoint — `Parser.decodeByteStringLiteral`),
// so this is a pure `.rdata` registration: identical literals dedup to one blob (`registerRdataConstant`).
export function registerByteArrayLiteralBlob(globalData GlobalDataTable, bytes ByteArray) returns String
	return globalData.registerRdataConstant(ByteArrayBlobPrefix, bytes: paddedByteArrayBlob(bytes))
end 'registerByteArrayLiteralBlob'

function paddedByteArrayBlob(bytes ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	var blob = ByteArray.create()
	blob.append(bytes)
	while blob.count() mod ByteArrayBlobAlignment != 0 'pad'
		blob.push(ByteArrayBlobPadByte)
	end 'pad'
	return blob
end 'paddedByteArrayBlob'

// ⭐ **THE ABORT CODES THIS FILE DIVERGES WITH ARE `RuntimeAbort`'s, NOT ITS OWN.** They used to be four
// file-local `let`s picked against a file-local list, which is how this file's element-size-zero code and
// `GtRuntime.ProcStoreOverflowExitCode` both came to be 70. The registry is one declaration site whose
// duplicate check is the language's (an enum's raw values must be distinct — E3031), so the collision
// cannot recur. Four aborts live here, each stopping an operation that must never proceed:
//
//   * `arrayElementSizeZero` — `__arr_create` handed a 0 element size, which is a COMPILER bug (a
//     concrete literal is never 0, a descriptor read never yields 0), mirroring v1's
//     `__mm_panic_element_size_zero`: a zero would silently make `__arr_grow` allocate a zero-byte buffer.
//   * `arrayCapacityOverflow` — `__arr_grow`'s `capacity·element_size` wrapped i64, so `__mm_alloc` would
//     hand back a buffer far too small for the capacity the record then claims and the next element write
//     would run off its end (a silent heap overflow). The reference compiler aborts here too
//     (`__ManagedMemory: realloc size must be > 0`); this is the same guard one arithmetic step earlier,
//     so it also catches a wrap to a small NON-zero value.
//   * `arrayAppendElementSizeMismatch` — `__arr_append`'s two records disagree on `element_size@24`.
//     `append` is the ONE array op that strides TWO records by a SINGLE element size (it reads `dest`'s
//     and applies it to `src`), so it is correct only when they agree. Same-static-type arrays normally
//     do; the ONE exception is a byte-string literal (`b"…"`), byte-PACKED at `element_size@24 == 1`
//     while a heap-grown `Array with Byte` strides by a machine word — blitting `srcLen ·
//     dest.element_size` bytes across that seam OVER-READS the packed source and splices garbage in.
//     Making such an append WORK (an element-wise WIDENING copy across differing strides) is a distinct
//     mechanism, not slice 4's.
//   * `arrayNegativeResize` — the one with a contract argument behind it, immediately below.

// `arrayNegativeResize` — `__arr_resize` asked for a NEGATIVE length. A length is a count, so a
// negative one is never a value the record may hold — and the reason this is an abort rather than a merely
// cosmetic wrong answer is that the shrink path zeroes `[n·es, oldLen·es)` starting at `buffer + n·es`,
// which for a negative `n` is an address BEFORE the buffer, erasing the `__mm_alloc` header that sits just
// below the payload. It is the one out-of-buffer write a DETACH cannot fix — detaching only moves the write
// to a different allocation — so it is rejected here, by the same "a corrupt operation must never proceed"
// guard the other three above are.
//
// ⚠ A BYTE-STRING LITERAL IS NOT A SEPARATE CASE HERE, and it is worth saying because it USED to be: before
// the COW slice the under-write landed in read-only `.rdata` and faulted loudly. It no longer does.
// `__arr_resize` reaches its shrink through `__arr_grow`, whose FIRST op is the unconditional detach, so by
// the time the zero range is computed the record is already on a private HEAP buffer and a literal corrupts
// the allocator exactly like an owned array — QUIETLY. Measured with this guard stubbed out: `b"hey"` then
// `resize(-2)` exits 0, the leak gate stays green, `capacity()` reads 3 (proving the detach fired) and
// `count()` publishes -2. A louder failure mode became a silent one, which is why the guard is here.
//
// ⭐ THE CANONICAL CONTRACT ALREADY SAYS SO, IN A TYPE: `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `resize(newLength ElementCount)`
// takes `ElementCount = int(0 to u64.max)` (`:11`), so a negative length is not a value the operation accepts
// — the reference stdlib expresses that as a RANGE and gets the rejection from the type system. shv2's builtin
// array methods pass their argument through as a plain integer (the same reason `get`/`set`/`remove` bounds-
// check a negative INDEX at runtime rather than inheriting `ElementIndex`'s range), so the range has to be
// re-asserted here. This guard is that assertion, in the one place shv2 can currently make it.
//
// ⚠ THE REFERENCE COMPILER once had no coherent answer here — it PANICKED on an owned array but silently
// published `count() == -2` for a byte-string literal, because its negative rdata capacity sentinel satisfied
// its own growth check and the assert was never reached. That bootstrap bug is FIXED as of `115fdcfc3`
// (#102): it now panics uniformly for both storage strategies. shv2 aborts (this exit code) uniformly, which
// agrees with the bootstrap's fixed behaviour on "a negative length is not an accepted value".

// The error flag `__arr_get`/`__arr_set`/`__arr_first`/`__arr_last`/`__arr_pop`/`__arr_remove` throw on an
// out-of-bounds index — the dual-register `errorReturn` flag (an error case's `ordinal + 1`, so 1 is
// `ArrayError.indexOutOfBounds`, ordinal 0). `try … otherwise` only tests `flag != 0`, so any nonzero
// value routes to the handler; the specific code is the honest ordinal for the day a handler binds it.
let ArrIndexOutOfBoundsFlag = 1 as ParsedInt
let NoErrorFlag = 0 as ParsedInt

// The growth policy — Go's `nextslicecap`, ported VERBATIM from `stdlib/Array.maxon:65-95`. Double below
// `GrowthThreshold`, then ease toward 1.25× above it.
//
// ⚠ IT BENDS THE SCALE-TEST'S BYTE COLUMN, AND SAYING SO ONCE IS CHEAPER THAN THE NEXT AGENT HUNTING A
// PHANTOM QUADRATIC (this comment is carried from stdlib/Array.maxon:14-55). Under plain doubling the
// bytes a buffer allocates over its life are a FLAT 2·N whatever N is. Here the ratio `r` is a function
// of the capacity — ×2.0 at the threshold, rising toward ×5 as it approaches 1.25× — so a phase whose
// bytes are perfectly LINEAR in the program reads ABOVE ×2.00 per doubling while it climbs. The scale
// ladder DOUBLES each rung, so buffers get bigger each rung and the multiple climbs each rung: a reading
// of ×2.1 on a linear phase is the EXPECTED cost of this policy, NOT a new quadratic. It saturates (the
// multiple stops at 5), so it is a TRANSIENT bend, not a superlinearity — but the ladder's six rungs sit
// inside the transition. (Do NOT reintroduce the phantom `max(req+1, cap·2, 64)` doubling the stdlib
// comment names: it no longer exists in the bootstrap.)
let GrowthThreshold = 256 as ParsedInt
let MinimumCapacity = 4 as ParsedInt

// The easing step is `capacity + (capacity + 3·GrowthThreshold)/4`. The `3` and the `4` are named (and
// the `3·GrowthThreshold` addend is derived from `GrowthThreshold` in the IR, not written as a second
// literal 768) so the formula reads as the policy and no fact is written twice.
let EaseNumeratorScale = 3 as ParsedInt
let EaseDivisor = 4 as ParsedInt

// The runtime entry points. `__`-prefixed (`isCompilerInternalCallee` routes their calls past the
// user-function checks — no file declares them, so no E3004, no signature slotting) but NOT `__mm_`-
// prefixed, so `isRuntimeCallee` leaves them out of the raw heap decision — instead `isArrayRuntimeCallee`
// carries both `usesArray` AND `usesHeap` (they `__mm_alloc`/`__mm_free` inside their Std bodies).
export let ArrCreateName = b"__arr_create"
export let ArrPushName = b"__arr_push"
export let ArrGetName = b"__arr_get"
export let ArrSetName = b"__arr_set"
export let ArrCountName = b"__arr_count"
export let ArrCapacityName = b"__arr_capacity"
export let ArrIsEmptyName = b"__arr_is_empty"
export let ArrReserveName = b"__arr_reserve"
export let ArrResizeName = b"__arr_resize"
export let ArrFirstName = b"__arr_first"
export let ArrLastName = b"__arr_last"
export let ArrPopName = b"__arr_pop"
export let ArrClearName = b"__arr_clear"
export let ArrInsertName = b"__arr_insert"
export let ArrRemoveName = b"__arr_remove"
export let ArrDecrefName = b"__arr_decref"

// The whole-array copy operations (P1.7 slice 4). `slice` is THROWING (bounds/inverted-range → `ArrayError`),
// `clone`/`append` are not. `slice`/`clone` return an array that BEHAVES as an independent copy but is an
// O(1) zero-copy VIEW over the source's buffer until either side writes (P1.7 COW slice B — see
// `emitArrayView`); `append` grows `self` and block-copies `other`'s bytes in, leaving `other` untouched.
// TRIVIAL elements only — a managed element would need a per-element deep clone (the parser rejects the
// managed cases, P1.7 slice 4).
export let ArrSliceName = b"__arr_slice"
export let ArrCloneName = b"__arr_clone"
export let ArrAppendName = b"__arr_append"

// Internal helpers the public ones call: `__arr_grown_cap` is the policy, `__arr_grow` reallocs to fit,
// `__arr_cow_detach` gives the record a private writable buffer before any in-place write (P1.7 COW slice A).
let ArrGrownCapName = b"__arr_grown_cap"
let ArrGrowName = b"__arr_grow"
let ArrCowDetachName = b"__arr_cow_detach"

// The bare error-enum NAME the seven throwing accessors' `throws` clause carries — the `E` of `throws E`,
// stamped on each throwing builder's `IrFunction` (`throwsClauseName`). ONE declaration so the string is not
// repeated seven times, and a `String` (not a `b"…"` ByteArray like the callee-name constants above) because
// `ThrowsClause.throwing` takes the type name as a String. It matches `ArrayError` in `stdlib/Array.maxon`,
// the enum a user `try array.get(i) otherwise …` binds; for the runtime functions it is documentation only —
// `funcSignatureResultValTypes` reads `throwsClauseIsThrowing` (throwing vs none), never the name's bytes.
let ArrayErrorTypeName = "ArrayError"

// The `__arr_` prefix that identifies EVERY array runtime entry point — the one test `scanRuntimeUsage`
// reads to set `usesArray` (and `usesHeap`).
let ArrayRuntimePrefix = b"__arr_"

export function isArrayRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: ArrayRuntimePrefix)
end 'isArrayRuntimeCallee'

// The THROWING array accessors — `get`/`set`/`first`/`last`/`pop`/`remove`/`slice` return via the dual-register
// `errorReturn` ABI and can throw `ArrayError.indexOutOfBounds`, so a call to one MUST become a `tryCall`
// (it is under a `try`, which `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` converts). SemanticCheck reads THIS predicate to
// report E3057 on a bare `call` to one — the same "throwing call needs `try`" rule it applies to a user
// function, which its blanket compiler-internal skip would otherwise hide. Without it the plain call reads
// only the value register and drops the error flag, so an out-of-bounds `get` silently returns 0 (a wrong
// answer, and a divergence from the reference compiler, which rejects it). THE ONE home of "which array
// callee throws": `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod` emits exactly these seven through its throwing path, and the
// two lists name the same seven `Arr*Name` constants so they cannot drift. `slice` (P1.7 slice 4) throws on
// an out-of-bounds or inverted range.
export function isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(ArrGetName) or callee.equals(ArrSetName) or callee.equals(ArrFirstName) or callee.equals(ArrLastName) or callee.equals(ArrPopName) or callee.equals(ArrRemoveName) or callee.equals(ArrSliceName)
end 'isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee'

// A byte width used to shift a byte into (or out of) its position in a little-endian element word. `i*8`
// is the bit offset of element byte `i`; `BitsPerByte` names the 8.
let BitsPerByte = 8 as ParsedInt

// Install the array runtime into the lowered Std module — ALL of it, when the program touches any array.
// Golden-neutral: `__`-prefixed functions are excluded from the spec goldens (`isRuntimeFunction`), so an
// all-or-nothing install costs nothing in the fragments and needs no per-helper dependency tracking; the
// helpers call each other (`push`→`grow`→`grown_cap`→`__mm_alloc`) so a dependency graph would only re-
// derive the closure this install already is. Appended AFTER the pipeline (like `installMmRuntime`), so
// they are never re-optimized.
export function installArrayRuntime(module StdModule, usage RuntimeUsage)
	if not usage.usesArray 'noArray'
		return
	end 'noArray'
	// `__mm_incref` is built by `installMmRuntime` (under `usesIncref || usesArray`), not here: the parser
	// now emits `__mm_incref` too (the `try … otherwise` phi reconciliation) in programs that touch no
	// array, so its install can no longer live behind `usesArray` alone — see that function's note.
	// `usesArray` implies `usesHeap` (`isArrayRuntimeCallee`), so `installMmRuntime` has already run.
	buildArrCowDetach(module)
	buildArrGrownCap(module)
	buildArrGrow(module)
	buildArrCreate(module)
	buildArrPush(module)
	buildArrGet(module)
	buildArrSet(module)
	buildArrCount(module)
	buildArrCapacity(module)
	buildArrIsEmpty(module)
	buildArrReserve(module)
	buildArrResize(module)
	buildArrFirst(module)
	buildArrLast(module)
	buildArrPop(module)
	buildArrClear(module)
	buildArrInsert(module)
	buildArrRemove(module)
	buildArrSlice(module)
	buildArrClone(module)
	buildArrAppend(module)
	buildArrDecref(module)
	verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate(module)
end 'installArrayRuntime'

// ⭐ **THE DRIFT GUARD for "which array callee throws".** `isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee` names seven
// accessors, and exactly seven builders above stamp a `throws ArrayError` clause; those two facts must stay
// the SAME seven or a callee's wasm SIGNATURE and its call site disagree — the throwing function gains (or
// loses) the i64 error-flag result `funcSignatureResultValTypes` derives from the clause, while the body and
// the call site use the other shape. That mismatch is INVISIBLE on native (the flag rides a fixed register
// outside the signature) and surfaces on wasm as an invalid module — precisely the Slice 1 bug this rung
// fixed. The predicate's header PROMISES the two lists cannot drift; this makes it a CHECKED invariant. It
// reads the ACTUAL stamped clause off each built function rather than re-listing the seven names (a re-list
// would be a third copy to drift), and checks BOTH directions: a `__arr_` function is throwing iff the
// predicate names it. Runs once per array-using compile (`installArrayRuntime`'s tail) — linear in the
// module's function count, a compile abort rather than a runtime trap on drift.
function verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate(module StdModule)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		if isArrayRuntimeCallee(func.name) 'arr'
			let stamped = throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName)
			let named = isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee(func.name)
			if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
				panic("installArrayRuntime: '{String.from(func.name)}' stamps a `throws ArrayError` clause but isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee does NOT name it — the throwing-accessor builder set and the predicate have drifted (P1.7 slice 1)")
			end 'stampedNotNamed'
			if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
				panic("installArrayRuntime: isThrowingArrayRuntimeCallee names '{String.from(func.name)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-accessor builder set and the predicate have drifted (P1.7 slice 1)")
			end 'namedNotStamped'
		end 'arr'
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate'

// --- The byte-loop builders: the runtime-polymorphic element_size lives HERE ---
//
// Every element move is byte-granular because `element_size` is a runtime field. Four primitives, each a
// header-guarded loop (the `buildStrCopy` shape), threaded through `cur`: they set `cur`'s terminator and
// return the continuation block (`emitLoadElem` also the loaded value). One implementation of each,
// shared by every accessor, so the element-stride arithmetic lives in exactly one place per direction.

// Copy `byteCount` bytes from `src` to `dest`, LOW address first — safe when `dest <= src` (a grow's
// old→new copy, a remove's shift-left). `byteCount == 0` copies nothing (the header guard holds at once).
function emitForwardCopy(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, dest ValueId, src ValueId, byteCount ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpyfh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpyfb")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpyfd")

	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: byteCount, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let srcAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(srcAddr, lhs: src, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let b = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(b, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let dstAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(dstAddr, lhs: dest, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: b, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	return done
end 'emitForwardCopy'

// Copy `byteCount` bytes from `src` to `dest`, HIGH address first — the overlap-safe direction when
// `dest > src` (an insert's shift-right). The cursor `i` counts DOWN from `byteCount` to 0; each step
// copies index `i-1`, so `i` doubles as the next iteration count.
function emitBackwardCopy(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, dest ValueId, src ValueId, byteCount ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpybh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpybb")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cpybd")

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(byteCount)))
	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let atStart = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atStart, lhs: i, rhs: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atStart, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let k = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(k, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let srcAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(srcAddr, lhs: src, rhs: k, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let b = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(b, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let dstAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(dstAddr, lhs: dest, rhs: k, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: b, valueType: StdType.u8))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(k)))

	return done
end 'emitBackwardCopy'

// ⭐ **THE MANAGED-ELEMENT DESTROY WALK (P1.7 slice 3).** Walk `[start, end)` of `buffer` (stride
// `elementSize`, always 8 for a managed element — a heap pointer) and DESTROY each NON-NULL slot by calling
// the `destroy` function pointer on it through a `callIndirect`. ONE home for the null-guarded per-slot
// destroy loop, shared by `__arr_decref` (drop, `[0, length)`), `__arr_clear` (`[0, length)`) and
// `__arr_resize`'s shrink (`[n, oldLen)`). The null-guard skips a MOVED-OUT slot (a `pop`/`remove` nulls the
// slot it hands to the caller). `destroy` is the element's own destroy function (`__str_decref` /
// `__destruct_<T>` / `__mm_decref` / `__arr_decref`), so each element is destroyed ONCE — shv2's move-only
// model, NOT v1's `&__mm_decref` refcount net. Threads through `cur` (sets its terminator) and returns the
// continuation block. Caller guards `destroy != 0` (a trivial-element array never reaches here).
function emitElementDestroyRange(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, buffer ValueId, start ValueId, stop ValueId, elementSize ValueId, destroy ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dtorh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dtorb")
	var doDrop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dtordo")
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dtorstep")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dtordone")

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(start)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: stop, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: body, buffer: buffer, index: i, elementSize: elementSize, ids: ids)
	let elem = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(elem, addrId: slot, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nonNull = emitCmp(module, block: body, a: elem, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(nonNull, thenBlockId: doDrop.id, elseBlockId: step.id))

	let dropped = ids.mint()
	// The element destructor is a genuinely VOID `(param i64)` function (`__str_decref`/`__destruct_<T>`/
	// `__mm_decref`/`__arr_decref`), so the call's `resultType` is `StdReturnType.void` — NOT `value(i64)`.
	// The distinction is INVISIBLE on native (an unused result register is simply not captured) but decides
	// the wasm `call_indirect` functype: `void` interns `(param i64)` with no result, matching the funcref's
	// real type, where `value(i64)` interned `(param i64)(result i64)` and TRAPPED the wasm type check. The
	// `dropped` id is the variant's required-but-dead result slot — never defined on a void call, never read.
	// The single element-pointer argument is never a float (mask 0).
	module.appendOp(doDrop, op: StdOp.callIndirect(dropped, calleeReg: destroy, resultType: StdReturnType.void, argFloatMask: 0 as ArgFloatMask, args: argsOf(elem)))
	module.setTerminator(doDrop, op: StdOp.branch(step.id))

	let one = emitConst(module, block: step, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iNext = emitBin(module, block: step, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	step.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	return done
end 'emitElementDestroyRange'

// ⭐ **GATE THE MANAGED-ELEMENT DESTROY WALK on `element_destroy@40` (P1.7 slice 3a).** Load the array's stamped
// element destructor and, when it is non-zero (a MANAGED-element array), DESTROY `[start, stop)` through
// `emitElementDestroyRange`; a TRIVIAL-element array (destructor 0) skips the walk untouched. Returns the block
// both paths converge on. This is the ONE home of the "must these slots be destroyed, and if so destroy them"
// decision — shared by `__arr_decref` (drop `[0, length)`), `__arr_set` (the ONE overwritten slot), `__arr_clear`
// (`[0, length)`) and `__arr_resize`'s shrink (`[n, oldLen)`) — so a trivial-element array can never
// `callIndirect(0)` and every mutator that VACATES a live managed slot destroys it exactly once instead of
// leaking it. Threads `cur` (sets its terminator); `record` is the array whose `element_destroy@40` is read,
// `buffer` its element storage.
function emitDestroyRangeIfManaged(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, buffer ValueId, start ValueId, stop ValueId, elementSize ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let dtor = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: ArrElementDestroyOffset, ids: ids)
	let zeroDtor = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let hasDtor = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: dtor, b: zeroDtor, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	var walk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mwalk")
	let cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mcont")
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasDtor, thenBlockId: walk.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))

	var afterWalk = emitElementDestroyRange(module, func: func, cur: walk, buffer: buffer, start: start, stop: stop, elementSize: elementSize, destroy: dtor, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterWalk, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	return cont
end 'emitDestroyRangeIfManaged'

// Store the low `size` bytes of the register `value` at `addr`, little-endian — a push/set/insert of a
// trivial element word. Byte `i` is `(value >>logical i·8)` stored as a `u8` (which keeps the low byte).
function emitStoreElem(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, addr ValueId, value ValueId, size ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"steh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"steb")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sted")

	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: size, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let bitsConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(bitsConst, value: BitsPerByte, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let shiftAmt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(shiftAmt, lhs: i, rhs: bitsConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let byteVal = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(byteVal, lhs: value, rhs: shiftAmt, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let dstAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(dstAddr, lhs: addr, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: byteVal, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))

	return done
end 'emitStoreElem'

// Load the `size` bytes at `addr` into a register word, zero-extended little-endian — a get/first/last/
// pop/remove of a trivial element. Byte `i` is `load_u8(addr+i) <<logical i·8`, OR-accumulated. On
// completion the loop branches to the caller-created `exit` block and the accumulated value is RETURNED
// (the loop-header accumulator, which the header dominates, so it is live on `exit` — the same domination
// `buildIntToString` relies on). The caller owns `exit` (a `var`), which is why it is passed IN rather
// than returned: a returned block cannot then be re-mutated by the caller under the ownership model.
function emitLoadElem(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, exit IrBlock, addr ValueId, size ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ldeh")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ldeb")

	let zero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(zero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let accZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.const(accZero, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))

	let i = ids.mint()
	let acc = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(acc, argType: StdType.i64))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: accZero)))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(header, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: size, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: exit.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	let srcAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(srcAddr, lhs: addr, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let b = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(b, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let bitsConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(bitsConst, value: BitsPerByte, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let shiftAmt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(shiftAmt, lhs: i, rhs: bitsConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let shifted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(shifted, lhs: b, rhs: shiftAmt, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let accNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(accNext, lhs: acc, rhs: shifted, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let one = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.const(one, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(body, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(iNext, second: accNext)))

	return acc
end 'emitLoadElem'

// Load the element at `addr` (size `size`) from array `record` and throw-RETURN it — the get/first/last tail.
// ⭐ **EMPTY-SLOT DETECTION for a MANAGED element (P1.7 slice 3).** A managed array grown by `resize` has NULL
// slots inside `[0, length)` (never written — the buffer is zeroed), and a null pointer is not a valid element:
// reading its fields would fault. So for a managed element (`element_destroy@40 != 0`) a NULL loaded value is
// treated as an empty slot and THROWS `indexOutOfBounds`, routing the caller's `try … otherwise` to its
// fallback. A TRIVIAL element's 0 is a genuine value (not empty), so the null-check is gated on the destructor
// and never fires for a trivial array — its ok-return is unchanged.
function emitLoadElemAndOkReturn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, addr ValueId, size ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var checkDtor = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gchkdtor")
	let value = emitLoadElem(module, func: func, cur: cur, exit: checkDtor, addr: addr, size: size, ids: ids)

	var checkNull = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gchknull")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gok")
	var empty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gempty")

	// checkDtor: a trivial element (destructor 0) skips the null-check; a managed one falls to checkNull.
	let dtor = emitLoadField(module, block: checkDtor, record: record, offset: ArrElementDestroyOffset, ids: ids)
	let zeroDtor = emitConst(module, block: checkDtor, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let managed = emitCmp(module, block: checkDtor, a: dtor, b: zeroDtor, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkDtor, op: StdOp.condBranch(managed, thenBlockId: checkNull.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	// checkNull: a NULL managed slot is empty → throw; a live one returns ok.
	let zeroVal = emitConst(module, block: checkNull, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isNull = emitCmp(module, block: checkNull, a: value, b: zeroVal, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkNull, op: StdOp.condBranch(isNull, thenBlockId: empty.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: empty, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: ok, value: value, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadElemAndOkReturn'

// Call `__arr_cow_detach(arr)` — the one obligation every in-place mutator owes before it touches the
// buffer (see `buildArrCowDetach` for the rule and for which mutators call this directly). The callee is
// void, so the minted result is unread; it exists because a `call` op always defines a value.
function emitCowDetach(module StdModule, block IrBlock, arr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let detached = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(detached, callee: ArrCowDetachName, args: argsOf(arr)))
end 'emitCowDetach'

// The element slot ADDRESS `buffer + index·elementSize` — the byte where element `index` starts.
function emitSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, buffer ValueId, index ValueId, elementSize ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let offset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: elementSize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: buffer, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSlotAddr'

// `value < 0` — the SIGNED-negative test. ⚠ shv2's `StdCmpPred` has NO unsigned compare, so a negative user
// index slips past every at-or-over-length bounds test (`index >= length` / `end > length`): signed, `-1 >= 3`
// is FALSE and the index reads as "in bounds", then `buffer + index·elementSize` addresses BEFORE the buffer
// (an OOB heap read/write). This explicit guard is the ONE home of "reject a negative index" — shared by
// `emitIndexOutOfRange` (get/set/remove) and `buildArrSlice`/`buildArrInsert`, so the fact is written once.
function emitIsNegative(module StdModule, block IrBlock, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: value, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
end 'emitIsNegative'

// A user index is OUT OF RANGE for a length-`length` array when it is NEGATIVE or at/above the length — the
// ONE home of the "reject this index" test `__arr_get`/`__arr_set`/`__arr_remove` share. The negative guard is
// OR-ed into the existing at-or-over-length test (both are 0/1 booleans, so the `bitOr` is a 0/1 boolean the
// `condBranch` to the OOB block truth-tests directly).
function emitIndexOutOfRange(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, length ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let negative = emitIsNegative(module, block: block, value: index, ids: ids)
	let atOrOver = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: index, b: length, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: negative, b: atOrOver, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitIndexOutOfRange'

// The out-of-bounds throw: `errorReturn(0, indexOutOfBounds)`. The primary value is unread on the throw
// edge (the desugar's phi takes the `otherwise` value there), so a plain 0 is honest.
function emitOobReturn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let zeroVal = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let flag = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ArrIndexOutOfBoundsFlag, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.errorReturn(zeroVal, errorFlagId: flag))
end 'emitOobReturn'

// A successful throwing return: `errorReturn(value, 0)`.
function emitOkReturn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let flag = emitConst(module, block: block, value: NoErrorFlag, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.errorReturn(value, errorFlagId: flag))
end 'emitOkReturn'

// ⭐⭐ **BUILD AN O(1) ZERO-COPY VIEW of `srcRecord`'s elements `[start, start+sliceLen)` (P1.7 COW slice B).**
// The shared body of `__arr_clone` (start 0, sliceLen = length) and `__arr_slice` (a bounds-checked sub-range).
//
// ⭐ **NOTHING IS COPIED, AND THE COST DOES NOT DEPEND ON `sliceLen`.** The result is a fresh 48-byte record
// (`__arr_create`, carrying the SAME `element_size` and `element_destroy` as the source) whose `buffer@0` is
// the INTERIOR pointer `srcBuffer + start·element_size`, whose `capacity@16` is `ViewBufferCapacity`,
// and whose `parent@32` is the shared allocation's BASE — increfed here, released by `__arr_cow_detach` or
// `__arr_decref`. That is ONE fixed-size allocation and no loop, where the previous eager copy allocated a
// second buffer of `sliceLen · element_size` bytes and moved every one of them.
//
// ⭐ **INDEPENDENCE IS PRESERVED BY DETACH-ON-WRITE, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS**, which is what lets the sharing be
// invisible: a write through the VIEW detaches it (`capacity < 0`), and a write through the OWNER detaches
// the owner (its buffer's refcount is non-zero). `slice-cow-modify-slice` and `slice-cow-modify-original`
// pin exactly those two directions, and they are unchanged by this — which is the point.
//
// ⭐ **NESTED SLICES FLATTEN.** `srcBuffer` is read straight out of the source, so slicing a VIEW windows
// within the view; and the base comes from `emitOwedAllocationBase`, which for a view is its own
// `parent@32`. So a slice of a slice increfs the ORIGINAL allocation and stores it directly — no chain
// exists, and nothing ever walks one.
//
// A base of 0 ("owes nothing") is ordinary and needs no special case beyond the incref's null guard: it is
// what a view over an immortal `.rdata` blob, or over an array that never allocated a buffer, correctly has.
//
// TRIVIAL elements only — the view duplicates heap POINTERS without a per-element retain, so a managed
// element would be destroyed twice; `requireTrivialArrayElementForCopy` rejects the managed cases (E2015).
// `exit` is caller-owned (passed IN, the `emitLoadElem` shape): the new record is minted in `cur`, which
// dominates `exit`, so it is live there for the caller's `ret`/`errorReturn`. Threads `cur` and returns the
// new record value.
function emitArrayView(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, exit IrBlock, srcRecord ValueId, start ValueId, sliceLen ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vwbase")

	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: srcRecord, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let dtor = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: srcRecord, offset: ArrElementDestroyOffset, ids: ids)
	let newRec = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(newRec, callee: ArrCreateName, args: twoArgs(es, second: dtor)))

	let srcBuffer = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: srcRecord, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let viewBuffer = emitSlotAddr(module, block: cur, buffer: srcBuffer, index: start, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: newRec, offset: ArrBufferOffset, value: viewBuffer)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: newRec, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: sliceLen)
	let sentinel = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: ViewBufferCapacity, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: newRec, offset: ArrCapacityOffset, value: sentinel)

	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: cur, join: baseJoin, record: srcRecord, slots: arrayBufferSlots(), ids: ids)

	// baseJoin: record the debt, then take out the second ownership it names.
	emitStoreField(module, block: baseJoin, record: newRec, offset: ArrParentOffset, value: base)
	var afterIncref = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, base: base, callee: MmIncrefName, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterIncref, op: StdOp.branch(exit.id))

	return newRec
end 'emitArrayView'

// --- The runtime functions ---

// ⭐ **`__arr_cow_detach(arr)` — GIVE THE RECORD A PRIVATE, WRITABLE BUFFER (P1.7 COW slice A).** If `arr`'s
// buffer is already privately owned and writable, do nothing; otherwise replace it with a fresh `__mm_alloc`'d
// copy of the array's LIVE bytes. Every in-place mutator calls this before its first buffer write, so a write
// can never reach memory the record does not own.
//
// ⭐ **THERE ARE TWO TRIGGERS, AND THEY ARE THE TWO DIRECTIONS OF SHARING (P1.7 COW slice B).** Slice A
// shipped the first and promised the second would arrive as another arm of THIS test, without moving the
// check or touching a call site; it did.
//
//  1. **`capacity@16 < 0` — the buffer is NOT this record's** (`emitBufferNotOwned`): a byte-string
//     literal's immortal `.rdata` blob, or a zero-copy VIEW's interior pointer into someone else's
//     allocation. Writing either in place is a fault or a corruption of another array's elements.
//  2. **`capacity@16 >= 0` AND THE BUFFER'S OWN REFCOUNT IS NON-ZERO — an OWNER whose bytes are being
//     VIEWED.** The record may write its own buffer in general; it may not while a view is reading it.
//
// ⭐ **ARM 2 IS THE HALF NEITHER REFERENCE HAS, AND IT IS WHY SHARING CAN BE REAL AT ALL.** A COW check
// that fires only for the viewer protects the parent from the slice and leaves the slice unprotected from
// the parent — a one-directional hole. The bootstrap's would-be equivalent, `maxon_cow_struct_detach`,
// tests `refcount != 1`, is emitted into every binary, and is CALLED FROM NOWHERE; v1's `cow_check` is
// likewise only ever reached from the copy's side. `slice-cow-modify-original` is the spec that says the
// hole is not acceptable, and this arm is what answers it: the parent detaches, keeps its own identity, and
// leaves the old bytes to the views that are still reading them.
//
// ⚠ **THE SENTINEL IS TESTED BEFORE ANY HEADER READ, AND THE ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING.** Arm 2 asks
// `emitIsSharedAllocation`, which reads a refcount that only exists when the pointer is one `__mm_alloc`
// RETURNED. A view's `buffer@0` is INTERIOR, so the word it would read is the parent's element DATA;
// asking arm 2 first would detach (or not) on the value of an element.
//
// ⭐ **THE CHECK LIVES HERE, NOT AT THE CALL SITES — and that is a deliberate departure from BOTH references.**
// The bootstrap emits its COW check per mutation site (`MaxonToStandardConversion.ManagedMemory.cs`) and v1
// emits it at each of its six, and both have holes because the list drifted: the bootstrap's `clear` and
// `setLength` mutate a shared buffer with no check at all, and v1's `shift_left`/`shift_right` are protected
// only by an arithmetic accident. shv2 has real emitted runtime functions that already take the record
// pointer, so putting the detach INSIDE them makes "every mutation site is gated" a property of the runtime
// rather than of a checklist a future mutator can be added without reading.
//
// ⭐ **THE GATING RULE, stated once: a mutator either calls `__arr_grow` before it touches the buffer, or it
// calls `__arr_cow_detach` itself.** `__arr_grow`'s FIRST op is an unconditional detach — placed ahead of its
// own `capacity >= required` early-out precisely so the guarantee does not depend on the growth arithmetic —
// so `push`/`reserve`/`resize`/`insert`/`append` are gated by construction through the call they already make,
// and `set`/`clear`/`remove`/`pop`, which write without ever growing, call it directly. Those five are the
// only call sites, and a sixth in (say) `insert` would be an unreachable branch: by the time it ran, `grow`
// would already have made `capacity >= 0`, so it could never do anything. `get`/`first`/`last`/`count`/
// `capacity`/`is_empty` are READS and must NOT detach; `__arr_append`'s `src` is borrowed and read-only, so
// only its `dest` is gated.
//
// ⚠ **THE TRAPS, each one already paid for elsewhere:**
//  1. **The buffer is loaded AFTER the detach, never before** — a pointer read ahead of the check is the
//     stale shared one, and the write goes to the buffer the record no longer uses.
//  2. **A ZERO-LENGTH array still detaches.** There is deliberately no `liveBytes == 0` early-out: skipping it
//     leaves the rdata pointer in place with the sentinel capacity, and the next reallocation then reads an
//     `__mm_alloc` header that does not exist. `__mm_alloc(0, 0)` is a legal empty box, so the general path
//     already handles it.
//  3. **The fresh buffer comes from `__mm_alloc`**, which zeroes — the `[length, capacity)`-reads-ZERO
//     invariant the whole file leans on. Here it is trivially satisfied (`capacity` is set to `length`, so
//     that range is empty), but the allocation must never become a raw non-zeroing one.
//  4. **`length · element_size` LIVE bytes are copied, not `capacity`'s worth** — and for a VIEW that is
//     exactly its own window, because `buffer@0` already points at the view's first element. There is no
//     `start` arithmetic here and there must not be: the record's `length@8` IS the window's length.
//  5. **The OLD buffer is RELEASED, NOT FREED, AND ONLY AFTER THE COPY.** It is released through
//     `emitOwedAllocationBase` + `emitReleaseOwedBase`, so an rdata blob (base 0) is left alone, a
//     view's parent is decref'd, and an owner detaching from its own viewed buffer gives up its share of
//     it. ⚠ The release MUST follow the copy: a view whose parent has already dropped is the LAST owner of
//     those bytes, so the decref frees them, and reading them afterwards is a use-after-free.
//
//     ⚠⚠ **AND NO TEST CAN CATCH A REVERSAL OF THAT ORDER — MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED.** Reversing it (release
//     emitted before `emitForwardCopy`) was built and run during review: the suite stayed 1197/0, because
//     `__mm_free` RECLAIMS NOTHING under the bump allocator (it only lowers the live count), so the freed
//     bytes read back intact. The ordering is therefore a RULE this function keeps on its own, not one a
//     gate protects — it is what makes the function still correct on the day `__mm_free` reuses memory, and
//     that is the day it also becomes testable. Do not "simplify" the two halves back together; the split
//     across the copy is the whole point of `base` being read at `detach` and spent at `afterCopy`.
//
// The published `capacity@16` is `length` — the buffer holds exactly the live elements and no slack, which is
// also what the reference compiler reports after it detaches (`b"hi"`, then `set`, then `capacity()` → 2) —
// and `parent@32` returns to `NoOwedAllocation`, because a record that has just copied its bytes out owes
// nothing to anyone.
function buildArrCowDetach(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrCowDetachName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var checkShared = module.addBlock(func, label: b"chkshared")
	var checkRc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"chkrc")
	var detach = module.addBlock(func, label: b"detach")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"basejoin")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	// entry — ARM 1: a buffer that is not this record's must be replaced before any write. Asked FIRST, so
	// the header read below is only ever reached on a pointer that HAS a header (see the ordering note).
	let notOwned = emitBufferNotOwned(module, block: entry, record: arr, slots: arrayBufferSlots(), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(notOwned, thenBlockId: detach.id, elseBlockId: checkShared.id))

	// checkShared: an owner with no buffer at all (an array that never grew) has nothing to share and
	// nothing to write; the mutator that follows will grow it.
	let ownBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: checkShared, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let zeroBuf = emitConst(module, block: checkShared, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let hasBuf = emitCmp(module, block: checkShared, a: ownBuf, b: zeroBuf, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkShared, op: StdOp.condBranch(hasBuf, thenBlockId: checkRc.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	// checkRc — ARM 2: the buffer is this record's, but is anyone else holding it? `emitIsSharedAllocation`
	// owns what the count means (`owners - 1`, so sole ownership is 0), and the only thing that can hold a
	// second reference to an element buffer is a VIEW — so "shared" here reads literally as "someone is
	// reading these bytes", and the write must go elsewhere.
	let viewed = emitIsSharedAllocation(module, block: checkRc, ptr: ownBuf, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkRc, op: StdOp.condBranch(viewed, thenBlockId: detach.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	// detach: read the allocation this record owes a release to BEFORE anything is republished — the answer
	// is derived from the very `capacity`/`buffer`/`parent` fields the publish below overwrites.
	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: detach, join: baseJoin, record: arr, slots: arrayBufferSlots(), ids: ids)

	// baseJoin: allocate a private buffer sized to the live bytes and copy them across.
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let liveBytes = emitBin(module, block: baseJoin, a: length, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let bufferDtor = emitConst(module, block: baseJoin, value: TrivialElementDestroy, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(baseJoin, op: StdOp.call(newBuf, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(liveBytes, second: bufferDtor)))
	let oldBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: baseJoin, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, dest: newBuf, src: oldBuf, byteCount: liveBytes, ids: ids)

	// afterCopy: publish the private buffer, then release the old allocation — in that order, and never
	// before the copy (see trap 5). `base` is a `baseJoin` block arg, and `baseJoin` dominates here.
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: arr, offset: ArrCapacityOffset, value: length)
	let noParent = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: NoOwedAllocation, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: arr, offset: ArrParentOffset, value: noParent)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: afterCopy, base: base, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrCowDetach'

// `__arr_grown_cap(current, required) -> newCap` — the growth policy. Ported verbatim from
// `stdlib/Array.maxon:grownCapacity`; see the `GrowthThreshold` note above for why it eases.
function buildArrGrownCap(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrGrownCapName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var takeAsk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"takeask")
	var checkSmall = module.addBlock(func, label: b"chksmall")
	var takeDoubled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"takedbl")
	var easeInit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"easeinit")
	var easeHeader = module.addBlock(func, label: b"easeh")
	var easeBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"easeb")
	var easeDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"eased")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let current = 0 as ValueId
	let required = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: an ask that outruns doubling is taken at face value.
	let doubled = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: current, b: current, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let overDouble = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: required, b: doubled, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(overDouble, thenBlockId: takeAsk.id, elseBlockId: checkSmall.id))

	emitAtLeastMinimumReturn(module, func: func, block: takeAsk, value: required, ids: ids)

	// checkSmall: double while below the threshold.
	let thresh = emitConst(module, block: checkSmall, value: GrowthThreshold, ids: ids)
	let isSmall = emitCmp(module, block: checkSmall, a: current, b: thresh, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkSmall, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSmall, thenBlockId: takeDoubled.id, elseBlockId: easeInit.id))

	emitAtLeastMinimumReturn(module, func: func, block: takeDoubled, value: doubled, ids: ids)

	// easeInit → easeHeader(cap = current): ease toward 1.25×.
	module.setTerminator(easeInit, op: StdOp.branch(easeHeader.id))
	let cap = ids.mint()
	easeHeader.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(cap, argType: StdType.i64))
	easeInit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(easeHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(current)))
	let needMore = emitCmp(module, block: easeHeader, a: cap, b: required, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(easeHeader, op: StdOp.condBranch(needMore, thenBlockId: easeBody.id, elseBlockId: easeDone.id))

	let threshConst = emitConst(module, block: easeBody, value: GrowthThreshold, ids: ids)
	let scaleConst = emitConst(module, block: easeBody, value: EaseNumeratorScale, ids: ids)
	let addend = emitBin(module, block: easeBody, a: threshConst, b: scaleConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let numerator = emitBin(module, block: easeBody, a: cap, b: addend, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let divisor = emitConst(module, block: easeBody, value: EaseDivisor, ids: ids)
	let step = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(easeBody, op: StdOp.div(step, lhs: numerator, rhs: divisor))
	let capNext = emitBin(module, block: easeBody, a: cap, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(easeBody, op: StdOp.branch(easeHeader.id))
	easeBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(easeHeader.id, argIds: argsOf(capNext)))

	module.setTerminator(easeDone, op: StdOp.ret(cap))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrGrownCap'

// Terminate `block` with `ret max(value, MinimumCapacity)` — the `atLeastMinimum` floor, emitted at each
// of `grownCapacity`'s two face-value returns so a small ask does not bounce up through 1→2→3.
function emitAtLeastMinimumReturn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var retFloor = module.addBlock(func, label: b"retfloor")
	var retVal = module.addBlock(func, label: b"retval")
	let floor = emitConst(module, block: block, value: MinimumCapacity, ids: ids)
	let below = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: value, b: floor, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(below, thenBlockId: retFloor.id, elseBlockId: retVal.id))
	let floorRet = emitConst(module, block: retFloor, value: MinimumCapacity, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(retFloor, op: StdOp.ret(floorRet))
	module.setTerminator(retVal, op: StdOp.ret(value))
end 'emitAtLeastMinimumReturn'

// `__arr_grow(arr, required)` — ensure the buffer holds at least `required` elements, reallocating (and
// copying the live bytes) when it does not. A fresh `__mm_alloc` is zeroed, and only `length·es` bytes
// are copied in, so the new `[length, newCap)` slots read zero — the capacity-slot invariant, for free.
function buildArrGrow(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrGrowName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"grow")
	var sizeOverflow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"overflow")
	var alloc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"alloc")
	let done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let required = 1 as ValueId

	// ⭐ **DETACH FIRST, UNCONDITIONALLY, AHEAD OF THE EARLY-OUT.** Every mutator that reaches the buffer
	// through a grow (`push`/`reserve`/`resize`/`insert`/`append`) is gated by THIS call, so it must not
	// depend on whether the growth check happens to fire — a `resize` whose `required` is below the rdata
	// sentinel would otherwise return from `done` with the record still pointing at read-only `.rdata`, and
	// the caller would write there. `capacity` is read AFTERWARDS because the detach republishes it.
	emitCowDetach(module, block: entry, arr: arr, ids: ids)

	// entry: already big enough ⇒ nothing to do.
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	let enough = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: capacity, b: required, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(enough, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: grow.id))

	// grow: pick the new capacity and verify `newCap·es` does not overflow the byte count — a wrapped small
	// value would under-allocate and let the next write run off the buffer (a heap overflow). `es >= 1`
	// (`__arr_create` diverges on a 0 element size), so the round-trip division is safe and equals `newCap`
	// exactly when the multiply did not wrap.
	let newCap = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.call(newCap, callee: ArrGrownCapName, args: twoArgs(capacity, second: required)))
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: grow, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let newBytes = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: newCap, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let roundTrip = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.div(roundTrip, lhs: newBytes, rhs: es))
	let overflowed = emitCmp(module, block: grow, a: roundTrip, b: newCap, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(grow, op: StdOp.condBranch(overflowed, thenBlockId: sizeOverflow.id, elseBlockId: alloc.id))

	// overflow: abort — a corrupt allocation must never be published.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: sizeOverflow, abort: RuntimeAbort.arrayCapacityOverflow, ids: ids)

	// alloc: allocate the new buffer and copy the live bytes forward.
	let zeroDtor = emitConst(module, block: alloc, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(alloc, op: StdOp.call(newBuf, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(newBytes, second: zeroDtor)))
	let oldBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: alloc, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let oldLen = emitLoadField(module, block: alloc, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let oldBytes = emitBin(module, block: alloc, a: oldLen, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: alloc, dest: newBuf, src: oldBuf, byteCount: oldBytes, ids: ids)

	// afterCopy: release the OLD buffer if there was one, then publish the new one. It needs no "is this
	// buffer MINE?" test: the detach at `entry` already replaced any non-owned buffer, so whatever is here
	// was `__mm_alloc`'d and this record owns it. That test used to live here as a second
	// `emitBufferNotOwned` in all but name; what remains is the ordinary null-guarded release every owner
	// owes on its base, which is why it is the shared `emitReleaseOwedBase` rather than a fourth copy —
	// an array that never grew simply has a base of 0. (Post-detach this record IS the last owner, so
	// `__mm_decref` and `__mm_free` are equivalent here today; using the one rule everywhere is what stops
	// them diverging tomorrow, and a mistaken decref leaks loudly where a mistaken free corrupts silently.)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: afterCopy, base: oldBuf, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: afterRelease, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterRelease, record: arr, offset: ArrCapacityOffset, value: newCap)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrGrow'

// `__arr_create(elementSize, elementDestroy) -> arrPtr` — a fresh empty array. The record comes zeroed
// from `__mm_alloc`, so only the two non-zero fields are stamped (`buffer`/`length`/`capacity`/`parent`
// are the zeroing contract's job); a 0 element size is a compiler bug and diverges (v1's guard).
function buildArrCreate(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrCreateName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var badSize = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badsize")
	var ok = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ok")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let elementSize = 0 as ValueId
	let elementDestroy = 1 as ValueId

	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isZeroSize = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: elementSize, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isZeroSize, thenBlockId: badSize.id, elseBlockId: ok.id))

	// This function RETURNS a record pointer, so its abort's dead terminator must carry a value — which
	// `emitRuntimeAbort` derives from `func.returnType` rather than being told. It used to be spelled out
	// here, on the argument that it was the file's ONE value-returning abort and a helper with a single
	// caller would only move it; `__gt_process_run`'s spawn failure was the second, in another file, and
	// that argument expired the moment the abort shape acquired a home of its own.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badSize, abort: RuntimeAbort.arrayElementSizeZero, ids: ids)

	let recordSize = emitConst(module, block: ok, value: ArrRecordBytes as ParsedInt, ids: ids)
	let recordDtor = emitConst(module, block: ok, value: TrivialElementDestroy, ids: ids)
	let record = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(ok, op: StdOp.call(record, callee: MmAllocName, args: twoArgs(recordSize, second: recordDtor)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: ok, record: record, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, value: elementSize)
	emitStoreField(module, block: ok, record: record, offset: ArrElementDestroyOffset, value: elementDestroy)
	module.setTerminator(ok, op: StdOp.ret(record))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrCreate'

// `__arr_push(arr, value)` — append `value`, growing first if the buffer is full.
function buildArrPush(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrPushName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let value = 1 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let required = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let grown = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(grown, callee: ArrGrowName, args: twoArgs(arr, second: required)))
	// Reload buffer AFTER grow (it may have replaced it); `length`/`es` are unchanged by a grow.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: entry, buffer: buffer, index: length, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	var afterStore = emitStoreElem(module, func: func, cur: entry, addr: slot, value: value, size: es, ids: ids)

	let newLen = emitBin(module, block: afterStore, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterStore, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: newLen)
	module.setTerminator(afterStore, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrPush'

// `__arr_get(arr, index) -> (value, flag)` — bounds-checked element read (throws indexOutOfBounds).
function buildArrGet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrGetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var load = module.addBlock(func, label: b"load")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let index = 1 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let outOfRange = emitIndexOutOfRange(module, block: entry, index: index, length: length, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(outOfRange, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: load.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: load, buffer: buffer, index: index, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	emitLoadElemAndOkReturn(module, func: func, cur: load, record: arr, addr: slot, size: es, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrGet'

// `__arr_set(arr, index, value) -> (_, flag)` — bounds-checked element write (throws indexOutOfBounds).
function buildArrSet(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrSetName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var store = module.addBlock(func, label: b"store")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let index = 1 as ValueId
	let value = 2 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let outOfRange = emitIndexOutOfRange(module, block: entry, index: index, length: length, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(outOfRange, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: store.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	// Detach BEFORE the buffer pointer is loaded (`__arr_cow_detach` trap 1), and after the bounds check, so a
	// throwing `set` — which writes nothing — allocates nothing.
	emitCowDetach(module, block: store, arr: arr, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: store, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: store, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	// ⭐ **DESTROY THE OCCUPANT `set` OVERWRITES (P1.7 slice 3a).** A managed array's `set` moves a new owner into
	// a LIVE slot, so the element it replaces must be destroyed first or it leaks (the parser consumes the new
	// value into the array — nothing else drops the old one). Destroy exactly the one slot `[index, index+1)`
	// through the shared gate (a trivial-element array skips it and stores directly), THEN store the new value.
	let one = emitConst(module, block: store, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let indexPlusOne = emitBin(module, block: store, a: index, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	var doStore = emitDestroyRangeIfManaged(module, func: func, cur: store, record: arr, buffer: buffer, start: index, stop: indexPlusOne, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: doStore, buffer: buffer, index: index, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	var afterStore = emitStoreElem(module, func: func, cur: doStore, addr: slot, value: value, size: es, ids: ids)
	let okVal = emitConst(module, block: afterStore, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: afterStore, value: okVal, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrSet'

// `__arr_count(arr) -> length`.
function buildArrCount(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrCountName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)
	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: 0 as ValueId, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(length))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrCount'

// `__arr_capacity(arr) -> capacity`.
function buildArrCapacity(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrCapacityName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)
	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let capacity = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: 0 as ValueId, offset: ArrCapacityOffset, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(capacity))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrCapacity'

// `__arr_is_empty(arr) -> bool` — length == 0, materialized to 0/1 through two return blocks (there is no
// `setcc` in the tier).
function buildArrIsEmpty(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrIsEmptyName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)
	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var yes = module.addBlock(func, label: b"yes")
	var no = module.addBlock(func, label: b"no")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: 0 as ValueId, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: yes.id, elseBlockId: no.id))
	let trueVal = emitConst(module, block: yes, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(yes, op: StdOp.ret(trueVal))
	let falseVal = emitConst(module, block: no, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(no, op: StdOp.ret(falseVal))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrIsEmpty'

// `__arr_reserve(arr, n)` — ensure capacity ≥ n. `__arr_grow` is a no-op when capacity already suffices.
function buildArrReserve(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrReserveName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)
	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let n = 1 as ValueId
	let grown = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(grown, callee: ArrGrowName, args: twoArgs(arr, second: n)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrReserve'

// `__arr_resize(arr, n)` — set length to n. Growing zero-fills the new tail (a grown buffer's `[length,
// newCap)` is already zero; an ungrown one's `[length, capacity)` is zero by the invariant), so only the
// SHRINK path must actively zero the vacated `[n, oldLen)` region to keep the invariant.
//
// ⚠ **A NEGATIVE `n` IS REJECTED, not clamped** (`RuntimeAbort.arrayNegativeResize`) — it is the one array write that
// lands OUTSIDE the buffer in both directions and that a detach cannot make safe, because the shrink's zero
// range starts at `buffer + n·es`. `StdCmpPred` is signed, so the existing `n < oldLen` shrink test happily
// accepts it; `emitIsNegative` is the file's one home for the guard that does not. It is a REJECT rather than
// `insert`'s clamp because `insert` documents a `[0, length]` clamping contract and `resize` documents none —
// a caller asking for a negative length has a bug, and silently resizing to empty would hide it.
function buildArrResize(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrResizeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var badLength = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badlen")
	var resize = module.addBlock(func, label: b"resize")
	var doZero = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dozero")
	var setLen = module.addBlock(func, label: b"setlen")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let n = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: a negative length can never be published (see the note above).
	let negative = emitIsNegative(module, block: entry, value: n, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(negative, thenBlockId: badLength.id, elseBlockId: resize.id))

	// badLength: abort — a negative length can never be published.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: badLength, abort: RuntimeAbort.arrayNegativeResize, ids: ids)

	let grown = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(resize, op: StdOp.call(grown, callee: ArrGrowName, args: twoArgs(arr, second: n)))
	let oldLen = emitLoadField(module, block: resize, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let shrink = emitCmp(module, block: resize, a: n, b: oldLen, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resize, op: StdOp.condBranch(shrink, thenBlockId: doZero.id, elseBlockId: setLen.id))

	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: doZero, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: doZero, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	// ⭐ **DESTROY THE VACATED MANAGED ELEMENTS `[n, oldLen)` BEFORE ZEROING (P1.7 slice 3a).** A shrink drops the
	// tail `[n, oldLen)`, so each managed element there must be destroyed or it leaks; the destroy MUST precede the
	// zero-fill (a zeroed slot is a NULL the walk would skip). A trivial-element array skips straight to the zero.
	var afterDestroy = emitDestroyRangeIfManaged(module, func: func, cur: doZero, record: arr, buffer: buffer, start: n, stop: oldLen, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	let startOff = emitBin(module, block: afterDestroy, a: n, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let endOff = emitBin(module, block: afterDestroy, a: oldLen, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let zeroBytes = emitBin(module, block: afterDestroy, a: endOff, b: startOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let startAddr = emitBin(module, block: afterDestroy, a: buffer, b: startOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	var afterZero = emitFillRange(module, func: func, cur: afterDestroy, dest: startAddr, byteCount: zeroBytes, fillValue: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterZero, op: StdOp.branch(setLen.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: setLen, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: n)
	module.setTerminator(setLen, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrResize'

// `__arr_first(arr) -> (value, flag)` — element 0 (throws on empty).
function buildArrFirst(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrFirstName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var load = module.addBlock(func, label: b"load")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: load.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	emitLoadElemAndOkReturn(module, func: func, cur: load, record: arr, addr: buffer, size: es, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrFirst'

// `__arr_last(arr) -> (value, flag)` — element length-1 (throws on empty).
function buildArrLast(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrLastName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var load = module.addBlock(func, label: b"load")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: load.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	let one = emitConst(module, block: load, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let lastIdx = emitBin(module, block: load, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: load, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: load, buffer: buffer, index: lastIdx, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	emitLoadElemAndOkReturn(module, func: func, cur: load, record: arr, addr: slot, size: es, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrLast'

// `__arr_pop(arr) -> (value, flag)` — remove and return the last element (throws on empty). The vacated
// slot is zeroed to keep `[length, capacity)` zero.
function buildArrPop(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrPopName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var pop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pop")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let isEmpty = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: length, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEmpty, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: pop.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	// Detach BEFORE the buffer pointer is loaded (`__arr_cow_detach` trap 1), and after the empty check, so a
	// throwing `pop` allocates nothing.
	emitCowDetach(module, block: pop, arr: arr, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: pop, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let lastIdx = emitBin(module, block: pop, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: pop, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: pop, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: pop, buffer: buffer, index: lastIdx, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	var afterLoad = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poploaded")
	let value = emitLoadElem(module, func: func, cur: pop, exit: afterLoad, addr: slot, size: es, ids: ids)
	var afterZero = emitFillRange(module, func: func, cur: afterLoad, dest: slot, byteCount: es, fillValue: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterZero, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: lastIdx)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: afterZero, value: value, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrPop'

// `__arr_clear(arr)` — length ← 0, zeroing the whole live region (capacity-slot invariant). The buffer
// and capacity are left in place for reuse.
function buildArrClear(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrClearName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	// Detach BEFORE the buffer pointer is loaded (`__arr_cow_detach` trap 1). `clear` cannot throw, and an
	// EMPTY literal detaches here too, into a zero-byte private buffer (trap 2).
	emitCowDetach(module, block: entry, arr: arr, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	// ⭐ **DESTROY THE LIVE MANAGED ELEMENTS `[0, length)` BEFORE ZEROING (P1.7 slice 3a).** `clear` drops the
	// whole live region, so each managed element must be destroyed or it leaks; the destroy MUST run before the
	// zero-fill (a zeroed slot is a NULL the walk would skip). A trivial-element array skips straight to the zero.
	let start = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	var afterDestroy = emitDestroyRangeIfManaged(module, func: func, cur: entry, record: arr, buffer: buffer, start: start, stop: length, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	let byteCount = emitBin(module, block: afterDestroy, a: length, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	var afterZero = emitFillRange(module, func: func, cur: afterDestroy, dest: buffer, byteCount: byteCount, fillValue: 0, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: afterZero, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterZero, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: zero)
	module.setTerminator(afterZero, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrClear'

// `__arr_insert(arr, index, value)` — insert at `index` (clamped to `[0, length]`), shifting the tail
// right by one slot (a backward byte copy, overlap-safe). Insert at `length` is a plain append.
//
// ⭐ **`insert` CLAMPS, it does NOT throw** — deliberately, mirroring the reference `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s
// `insert` ("clamps index to [0, count]"), which is why it stays a NON-throwing void mutator (unlike the
// throwing `get`/`set`/`remove`/`slice`, whose out-of-range index is an ERROR). So an out-of-range index is
// CLAMPED both ways: above `length` → append at `length`; BELOW 0 → insert at the front (0). The low clamp is
// not cosmetic — without it a negative index reaches `emitSlotAddr` and addresses `buffer + index·elementSize`
// BEFORE the buffer (an OOB heap WRITE, plus a backward-copy of `length - index` — a wrong count — slots).
// `StdCmpPred` is signed, so `index < 0` catches it; the pre-existing `index > length` never could (`-1 > 3`
// is FALSE). This makes the implementation honor its own long-documented `[0, length]` clamp contract.
function buildArrInsert(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrInsertName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var clampHigh = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clamphigh")
	var checkLow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"chklow")
	var clampLow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"clamplow")
	var useIndex = module.addBlock(func, label: b"useidx")
	var afterClamp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"aclamp")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let index = 1 as ValueId
	let value = 2 as ValueId

	// entry: an index above `length` clamps to `length`; otherwise fall to the low-side check.
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let overLen = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: index, b: length, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(overLen, thenBlockId: clampHigh.id, elseBlockId: checkLow.id))

	let clamped = ids.mint()
	afterClamp.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(clamped, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(clampHigh, op: StdOp.branch(afterClamp.id))
	clampHigh.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(afterClamp.id, argIds: argsOf(length)))

	// checkLow: a negative index clamps to the front (0); an in-range index is used as-is.
	let negative = emitIsNegative(module, block: checkLow, value: index, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkLow, op: StdOp.condBranch(negative, thenBlockId: clampLow.id, elseBlockId: useIndex.id))

	let frontIndex = emitConst(module, block: clampLow, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(clampLow, op: StdOp.branch(afterClamp.id))
	clampLow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(afterClamp.id, argIds: argsOf(frontIndex)))

	module.setTerminator(useIndex, op: StdOp.branch(afterClamp.id))
	useIndex.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(afterClamp.id, argIds: argsOf(index)))

	// afterClamp: grow for one more, then shift `[clamped, length)` up one slot and store `value`.
	let one = emitConst(module, block: afterClamp, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let required = emitBin(module, block: afterClamp, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let grown = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterClamp, op: StdOp.call(grown, callee: ArrGrowName, args: twoArgs(arr, second: required)))
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: afterClamp, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: afterClamp, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: afterClamp, buffer: buffer, index: clamped, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	let destAddr = emitBin(module, block: afterClamp, a: slot, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let moveCount = emitBin(module, block: afterClamp, a: length, b: clamped, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let moveBytes = emitBin(module, block: afterClamp, a: moveCount, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	var afterShift = emitBackwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: afterClamp, dest: destAddr, src: slot, byteCount: moveBytes, ids: ids)
	var afterStore = emitStoreElem(module, func: func, cur: afterShift, addr: slot, value: value, size: es, ids: ids)
	let newLen = emitBin(module, block: afterStore, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterStore, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: newLen)
	module.setTerminator(afterStore, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrInsert'

// `__arr_remove(arr, index) -> (value, flag)` — remove and return element `index` (throws OOB), shifting
// the tail left by one slot (a forward byte copy) and zeroing the vacated last slot.
function buildArrRemove(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrRemoveName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var remove = module.addBlock(func, label: b"remove")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let index = 1 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let outOfRange = emitIndexOutOfRange(module, block: entry, index: index, length: length, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(outOfRange, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: remove.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	// Detach BEFORE the buffer pointer is loaded (`__arr_cow_detach` trap 1), and after the bounds check, so a
	// throwing `remove` allocates nothing.
	emitCowDetach(module, block: remove, arr: arr, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: remove, record: arr, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: remove, record: arr, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSlotAddr(module, block: remove, buffer: buffer, index: index, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	var afterLoad = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rmloaded")
	let value = emitLoadElem(module, func: func, cur: remove, exit: afterLoad, addr: slot, size: es, ids: ids)

	// afterLoad: shift `[index+1, length)` down one slot into `slot`.
	let srcAddr = emitBin(module, block: afterLoad, a: slot, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: afterLoad, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let idxPlusOne = emitBin(module, block: afterLoad, a: index, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let remaining = emitBin(module, block: afterLoad, a: length, b: idxPlusOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let moveBytes = emitBin(module, block: afterLoad, a: remaining, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	var afterShift = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: afterLoad, dest: slot, src: srcAddr, byteCount: moveBytes, ids: ids)

	// afterShift: length--, then zero the now-unused last slot.
	let newLen = emitBin(module, block: afterShift, a: length, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterShift, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: newLen)
	let vacAddr = emitSlotAddr(module, block: afterShift, buffer: buffer, index: newLen, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	var afterZero = emitFillRange(module, func: func, cur: afterShift, dest: vacAddr, byteCount: es, fillValue: 0, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: afterZero, value: value, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrRemove'

// `__arr_slice(arr, start, end) -> (newArrPtr, flag)` — a new array over elements `[start, end)`, observably
// independent and built in O(1) as a zero-copy view (throwing). The range is out of bounds — throws `ArrayError.indexOutOfBounds` — when `end > length` OR
// `start > end` OR either index is NEGATIVE. `end > length` / `start > end` mirror the reference compiler's
// `LowerManagedMemSlice` (and `stdlib/Array.maxon`'s `slice`) and together also catch `start > length` (if
// `start <= end` then `end >= start > length` trips `end > length`; otherwise `start > end` trips), so
// `slice(5, endIndex: 5)` on a length-3 array throws via `end > length`. The explicit `start < 0` / `end < 0`
// guards are what stop a NEGATIVE index — signed compares never catch it (`end > length` is `-1 > 3` = FALSE,
// and `start > end` misses `slice(-1, endIndex: 2)` since `-1 > 2` is FALSE), so it would otherwise address
// `srcBuffer + start·elementSize` BEFORE the buffer (an OOB read). `emitArrayView` builds the result.
//
// ⭐ **A VIEW'S BOUNDS ARE ITS OWN `length@8`, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS A SLICE FROM BECOMING AN OOB READ.**
// `get`/`set`/`first`/`last`/`remove` bounds-check against the record they are handed and stride from that
// record's `buffer@0` — which for a view is already its first element — so `sub.get(i)` can address the
// window and nothing else. `sub` cannot see its parent's tail even though the bytes are right there.
//
// ⚠ **AN EMPTY SLICE (`start == end`, in bounds) SHARES like any other**, rather than short-circuiting to a
// fresh empty array: it is a length-0 view holding one reference to the parent's allocation. That is the
// deliberate choice — a special case here would be a second construction of a record to keep correct, and
// the general path is already right (its `emitSlotAddr` is `base + start·es`, never dereferenced at length
// 0, and its `parent@32` is simply 0 when the source never allocated a buffer). The cost is that an empty
// slice keeps its parent's bytes alive until it drops, which no program can observe.
function buildArrSlice(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrSliceName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params(), returnLine: 0, throwsTypeName: ThrowsClause.throwing(ArrayErrorTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var oob = module.addBlock(func, label: b"oob")
	var view = module.addBlock(func, label: b"view")
	var ret = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ret")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId
	let start = 1 as ValueId
	let endIndex = 2 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let endTooLarge = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: endIndex, b: length, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let startPastEnd = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: start, b: endIndex, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let startNegative = emitIsNegative(module, block: entry, value: start, ids: ids)
	let endNegative = emitIsNegative(module, block: entry, value: endIndex, ids: ids)
	let badRange = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: endTooLarge, b: startPastEnd, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let negativeIndex = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: startNegative, b: endNegative, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let anyErr = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: badRange, b: negativeIndex, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let violated = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: anyErr, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(violated, thenBlockId: oob.id, elseBlockId: view.id))

	emitOobReturn(module, block: oob, ids: ids)

	let sliceLen = emitBin(module, block: view, a: endIndex, b: start, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let newRec = emitArrayView(module, func: func, cur: view, exit: ret, srcRecord: arr, start: start, sliceLen: sliceLen, ids: ids)
	emitOkReturn(module, block: ret, value: newRec, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrSlice'

// `__arr_clone(arr) -> newArrPtr` — an observably independent copy of the whole array (non-throwing), built
// in O(1) as a zero-copy view that detaches at the first write from either side. It is `slice(0, length)` —
// always in bounds — so it reuses `emitArrayView` directly with `start = 0`, `sliceLen = length` and needs
// no bounds check (the reference `Array.clone` is literally `slice(0, len)`).
function buildArrClone(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrCloneName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var ret = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ret")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let arr = 0 as ValueId

	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: arr, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let start = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let newRec = emitArrayView(module, func: func, cur: entry, exit: ret, srcRecord: arr, start: start, sliceLen: length, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ret, op: StdOp.ret(newRec))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrClone'

// `__arr_append(dest, src)` — append `src`'s elements to `dest`; `dest` grows, `src` is READ-ONLY (borrowed and
// preserved, `append-preserves-originals`). Reserve `dest.length + src.length` up front (one grow, never per-
// element), then block-copy `src.length · element_size` bytes past `dest`'s live end. TRIVIAL elements only — a
// raw byte copy duplicates heap pointers, so a managed element would leave both arrays owning the same elements
// (a double-free); the parser rejects the managed cases. `dest.buffer` is reloaded AFTER the reserve because a
// grow may have replaced it. An empty `src` copies nothing (the forward-copy header guard holds at once).
//
// ⚠ **THE ELEMENT-SIZE SEAM.** The blit strides BOTH records by `dest`'s runtime `element_size`, so it is only
// coherent when `src`'s element size equals it. Same-static-type arrays normally agree; a byte-string literal
// (`element_size@24 == 1`, byte-packed) mixed with a machine-word `Array with Byte` does NOT, and blitting
// across that seam over-reads the packed source. `entry` guards the two element sizes equal and diverges
// (`RuntimeAbort.arrayAppendElementSizeMismatch`) otherwise — the file's "a corrupt operation must never proceed" shape.
function buildArrAppend(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrAppendName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var mismatch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"esmismatch")
	var append = module.addBlock(func, label: b"append")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let dest = 0 as ValueId
	let src = 1 as ValueId

	// entry: the two records must stride by the same element size (see THE ELEMENT-SIZE SEAM above).
	let destEs = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: dest, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let srcEs = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: src, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let esDiffers = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: destEs, b: srcEs, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(esDiffers, thenBlockId: mismatch.id, elseBlockId: append.id))

	// mismatch: abort — an incoherent cross-stride blit must never proceed.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: mismatch, abort: RuntimeAbort.arrayAppendElementSizeMismatch, ids: ids)

	let destLen = emitLoadField(module, block: append, record: dest, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let srcLen = emitLoadField(module, block: append, record: src, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let newLen = emitBin(module, block: append, a: destLen, b: srcLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let reserved = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(append, op: StdOp.call(reserved, callee: ArrReserveName, args: twoArgs(dest, second: newLen)))

	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: append, record: dest, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let destBuffer = emitLoadField(module, block: append, record: dest, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let srcBuffer = emitLoadField(module, block: append, record: src, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let destAddr = emitSlotAddr(module, block: append, buffer: destBuffer, index: destLen, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	let byteCount = emitBin(module, block: append, a: srcLen, b: es, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: append, dest: destAddr, src: srcBuffer, byteCount: byteCount, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: dest, offset: ArrLengthOffset, value: newLen)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrAppend'

// `__arr_decref(arr)` — an array's drop: the shared refcount RMW, then on the last owner DESTROY each managed
// element, free the external element buffer (if any) and the record.
//
// ⭐ **THE MANAGED-ELEMENT WALK (P1.7 slice 3).** When `element_destroy@40 != 0` the array's elements are
// managed heap POINTERS (a String / struct / union / instance / nested-Array box), so before the buffer is
// freed the last owner WALKS `[0, length)` and calls `element_destroy@40` on each NON-NULL slot through a
// `callIndirect`. ⭐ **The shv2 departure from v1:** `element_destroy@40` holds the element's DESTROY function
// (`__str_decref` / `__destruct_<T>` / `__mm_decref` / `__arr_decref`), NOT v1's `&__mm_decref` — shv2 is
// MOVE-ONLY (a slot holds the sole owner's +0 reference), so the walk destroys each element ONCE, it does not
// net a refcount. The null-guard skips a MOVED-OUT slot (a `pop`/`remove` nulls the slot it hands out) and a
// never-written `[length, capacity)` slot the walk never reaches (it strides only `[0, length)`, and the
// capacity-slot invariant keeps those zero anyway). A TRIVIAL-element array has `element_destroy@40 == 0` and
// takes the direct buffer free unchanged (no golden moves — the array helpers are `__`-prefixed, out of the
// spec fragments, so this whole restructuring is invisible to them).
//
// ⭐ **THE BUFFER IS RELEASED THROUGH THE SENTINEL LADDER, NOT FREED DIRECTLY** (P1.7 slices 2 and COW B).
// `emitOwedAllocationBase` answers which allocation — if any — this record owes a `__mm_decref` to, and
// `emitReleaseOwedBase` pays it. That pair covers all three record kinds and is the same pair
// `__arr_cow_detach` uses, so the two cannot disagree about what a record owns:
//
//   * an OWNER's base is its own `buffer@0` — `__mm_decref` reclaims it at the LAST owner, so a buffer still
//     under a view survives its parent record's death and the view keeps reading valid bytes;
//   * a VIEW's base is `parent@32` — the shared allocation, released here; its own interior `buffer@0` is
//     never freed, and must not be, since `__mm_free` wants the pointer `__mm_alloc` returned;
//   * a byte-string LITERAL's base is 0 — the `.rdata` blob was never `__mm_alloc`'d, so nothing is owed and
//     nothing is freed (`__mm_free`ing a `.rdata` address faults).
//
// ⚠ v1 paid for the twin of this three times (`stdlib/Internals.maxon:3465-3472`): a new non-owned-buffer
// kind must be taught to the DROP path, not only to the write check. The ladder is shared precisely so that
// teaching it once is enough — a literal that was MUTATED detached first and is now an owner with a private
// buffer; one that was only READ still owes nothing.
//
// ⚠ The managed-element walk runs UNCONDITIONALLY (behind its own `element_destroy@40` gate) rather than
// only for the last owner of the buffer, and that is sound rather than lucky: a managed-element array can
// never be SHARED, because `requireTrivialArrayElementForCopy` rejects `slice`/`clone`/`append` on one
// (E2015) and those are the only operations that mint a view. Its buffer's refcount is therefore always 0
// here. Gating the walk on that would be a branch no program can execute, which is the one thing this
// runtime consistently refuses to emit; the day managed elements can be viewed, this is the note that says
// what has to change.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE TWO HALVES OF THAT ARGUMENT ARE ONE FACT, NOT TWO THAT HAPPEN TO AGREE** — which is the only
// reason it may rest on a comment. The runtime gate here fires on `element_destroy@40 != 0`, and the parser
// gate rejects `slice`/`clone`/`append`; BOTH are `SignatureIndex.arrayElementIsManaged(giid)`, read at
// `Parser.arrayElementDestroyValue` (which stamps the field) and at `requireTrivialArrayElementForCopy`
// (which raises E2015). There is no third spelling to drift. ⚠ So the thing that would break this is NOT
// someone editing one copy — it is LIFTING the E2015 restriction (P1.7 slice 3b's deep-clone primitive)
// without also teaching the walk to run only for the buffer's last owner. Failure mode: a double destroy of
// every element under the shorter of two windows. Wrong answer, not an error.
function buildArrDecref(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(ArrDecrefName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lower = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lower")
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release")
	var releaseBuf = module.addBlock(func, label: b"relbuf")
	var baseJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"basejoin")
	var freeRec = module.addBlock(func, label: b"freerec")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0 as ValueId

	emitRefcountCheckToLastOwner(module, entry: entry, lower: lower, record: record, lastOwner: release, ids: ids)

	// release: destroy the live managed elements `[0, length)` before the buffer goes (the shared gate skips a
	// trivial-element array). An array that never allocated a buffer has length 0, so the walk is empty and
	// needs no separate guard.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: release, record: record, offset: ArrBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: release, record: record, offset: ArrLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let es = emitLoadField(module, block: release, record: record, offset: ArrElementSizeOffset, ids: ids)
	let start = emitConst(module, block: release, value: 0, ids: ids)
	var afterWalk = emitDestroyRangeIfManaged(module, func: func, cur: release, record: record, buffer: buffer, start: start, stop: length, elementSize: es, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterWalk, op: StdOp.branch(releaseBuf.id))

	let base = emitOwedAllocationBase(module, func: func, cur: releaseBuf, join: baseJoin, record: record, slots: arrayBufferSlots(), ids: ids)
	var afterRelease = emitReleaseOwedBase(module, func: func, cur: baseJoin, base: base, callee: MmDecrefName, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterRelease, op: StdOp.branch(freeRec.id))

	let recFreed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(freeRec, op: StdOp.call(recFreed, callee: MmFreeName, args: argsOf(record)))
	module.setTerminator(freeRec, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildArrDecref'
